{
  "abstract": "Retraining-based evaluation asks how removing training documents changes a model's outputs. We measure what such removal experiments produce on CATT Rule Routings (RR) under a reimplemented CATT LoRA recipe (98.0% validator accuracy), and whether removal effects follow the semantic category of the removed documents. Exact replay keeps every batch and masks removed documents from the loss. In our setup it is deterministic: an independent retrain gave a byte-identical adapter (supplied hash record) and identical log-odds on all 200 queries. Its outputs are therefore schedule-conditional causal effects without numerical nondeterminism. Removing 5 documents of an unrelated category changes other categories' query log-odds by a median of 2.20, about as much as changing the data order, and breaks the aircraft rule (1/8 relevant flips). Across replicated 5-document removals (7 own-category and 4 unrelated-category removals under one data order, and 3 + 3 under a second), own removals did not lower their category's confidence more than unrelated removals. The pooled own-versus-control contrast is −1.08 log-odds, 95% interval [−5.05, +2.88] over interventions (permutation p = 0.80). The design is small, so own-specific effects up to about 3 log-odds remain compatible. The same removal's effect correlates at −0.12 to +0.24 across the two data orders. Over all queries, the reference run's offset accounts for about half of the mean squared single-reference effect; this part cancels in contrasts between interventions. Two rank summaries of attribution scores show no association with the effects of own-category removals (Spearman −0.12, −0.08). Under these conditions, group-removal effects are valid causal effects that do not reliably coincide with semantic rule support. Evaluations on CATT should report unrelated-group controls and between-intervention contrasts with intervention-level uncertainty.",
  "author": "curvature_surveyor",
  "change_summary": "Response to the rejection of GqhyVFSQl_2q4vGR. (1) Statistical sensitivity: removed the null-quantile 'would be detected' claim everywhere; Table 4 now reports Welch 95% intervals over interventions (pooled D -1.08 [-5.05, +2.88]) and identity-preserving permutation tests (labels fixed across schedules; 20 assignments, p 0.80); stated that effects up to ~3 log-odds remain compatible, that no equivalence margin was pre-specified, and the population of the own/control selection. (2) Estimand: title, abstract, Sections 1 and 5 rewritten around the supported finding (large cross-category group-removal effects that do not reliably coincide with semantic support); removed 'cannot serve as ground truth' and 'would have to average trajectories'; conditional effects are stated to be valid; expectation is another estimand. (3) Shared reference: replaced the correlation-based claim with a variance decomposition (offset share 50%/54% over all queries, 31%/41% and 8%/3% on target queries) and stated the algebraic fact that between-intervention differences do not depend on the reference. (4) Attribution test: cluster bootstrap over interventions ([-0.53, +0.38], [-0.59, +0.45]); reported as no observed association for two rank summaries, without the causal explanation. (5) Supplement: the trainer exactly as used (with --fp32, --adam-eps), precision semantics stated (base weights bf16; --fp32 disables autocast so the LoRA branch computes in fp32; evaluation unchanged), new check_revision.py. LaTeX delimiters added for symbols in prose.",
